import{_ as a,o as s,c as t,an as n}from"./chunks/framework.B8uNL-A_.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Internals","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"internals.md","filePath":"internals.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"internals.md"};function r(o,e,c,l,d,p){return s(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n(`<h1 id="Internals" tabindex="-1">Internals <a class="header-anchor" href="#Internals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Internals {#Internals}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>How <a href="./perf/kernel_fusion">kernel fusion</a>, <a href="./perf/accelerate"><code>@accelerate</code></a> and memory management work under the hood.</p><h2 id="Broadcast-fusion" tabindex="-1">Broadcast fusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#Broadcast-fusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Broadcast fusion {#Broadcast-fusion}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Julia builds a nested <code>Broadcasted</code> tree for a dotted expression. If fusion is enabled, a GPU is present and the tree is eligible, cuNumeric flattens it, generates a CUDA.jl kernel and launches it as a Legate task. Otherwise each operation dispatches as a single cuPyNumeric operation in the Legate runtime.</p><p>Usage: <a href="./perf/kernel_fusion">Kernel Fusion</a>. Source: <code>src/ndarray/broadcast.jl</code>, <code>src/ndarray/broadcast_fusion.jl</code>.</p><h2 id="@accelerate" tabindex="-1"><code>@accelerate</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#@accelerate" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;\`@accelerate\` {#@accelerate}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>@accelerate</code> rewrites code before it runs. Take:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">@accelerate</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> function</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> f!</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(C, A, B)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    tmp </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> A </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.+</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> B          </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># a temporary, used once</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">    C .</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> tmp </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.*</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 2</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">    return</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> C</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">end</span></span></code></pre></div><ol><li><p><strong>Vertical fusion.</strong> <code>tmp</code> is used only once, so it is substituted into the next line: <code>C .= (A .+ B) .* 2</code>. Two kernels become one and <code>tmp</code> is never allocated.</p></li><li><p><strong>Early frees.</strong> Temporaries that can&#39;t be substituted (used twice, or fusion is off) get a free call inserted right after their last use. Arguments and returned values are never freed.</p></li></ol><p><code>@show_lifetimes</code> prints the rewritten code.</p><p>The compiler parses the body once into operation and value records. A <code>Graphs.SimpleDiGraph</code> connects producers to consumers and records ordering constraints around effects. Operands remain ordered lists, so <code>f(x, x)</code> counts as two uses. Reassigning a name creates a new value ID; aliases retain the old ID. Generated locals keep both values addressable until their last use.</p><p>Fusion updates these records and their execution schedule. Under <code>aggressive=true</code>, eligible adjacent indexed updates become a sibling launch. Alias and read-freshness guards select between separate optimized and original execution plans. Each plan receives its own cleanup placement.</p><p>A second directed graph connects lifetime parents to borrowing results, such as an array and its lazy transpose. After fusion, the compiler propagates last uses and escapes backward through this graph. It emits one deletion for each remaining locally owned, nonescaping array. The graphs and binding maps exist only during expansion; execution contains ordinary Julia operations, existing alias guards, and deletion calls.</p><p>Unknown calls keep their results and inputs until scope exit. If their result escapes, its inputs stay protected too. The compiler assumes that such calls do not retain arguments through side effects; it does not inspect function bodies. Borrowing declarations are internal implementation details, including registrations supplied by package extensions.</p><p>For expansion measurements, run <code>julia --project scripts/benchmark_accelerate.jl</code>. A Julia 1.10.12 comparison against <code>develop</code> (<code>8ddb229a</code>, fetched 2026-10-19) used identical input syntax and stand-in backend types, with each compiler in a separate process, five warmups and 30 samples. Minimum expansion time and allocations for a four-operation chain changed from 0.52 ms / 172,280 bytes to 0.49 ms / 170,584 bytes. The Gray–Scott test function changed from 31.59 ms / 9,180,224 bytes to 16.18 ms / 5,132,488 bytes. These are expansion measurements, not GPU execution benchmarks; timings depend on the environment. The scalar accelerated function (<code>y = x + 1; y * 2</code>) allocates zero runtime bytes on both develop and the graph compiler. The previously reported 1,040-byte saving was against the experimental ownership implementation on this feature branch (<code>9dbda372</code>), not against develop.</p><p>A same-process Julia 1.13.1/A30 comparison evaluated both compilers&#39; emitted functions against the same backend. After warmup, ten alternating batches with blocking execution fences measured a 100,000-element chain at 0.204 ms on develop and 0.209 ms with the graph compiler; a 100×100 Gray–Scott step measured 2.306 ms and 2.286 ms respectively. Batches used 100 chain calls or 30 steps. Julia allocations per call were 2,336 bytes for both chains, and 34,672 versus 35,728 bytes for Gray–Scott. This does not establish a material execution speedup; the demonstrated performance benefit is expansion time.</p><p>Usage: <a href="./perf/accelerate"><code>@accelerate</code></a>. Source: <code>src/scoping/</code>.</p><h2 id="Memory-and-GC" tabindex="-1">Memory and GC <a class="header-anchor" href="#Memory-and-GC" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Memory and GC {#Memory-and-GC}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An <code>NDArray</code> is a small Julia object pointing at storage owned by Legate, so Julia&#39;s GC doesn&#39;t see how much memory arrays use. cuNumeric tracks the bytes it allocates and frees. When that total exceeds 80% (soft limit) or 90% (hard limit) of device or host memory, it triggers Julia&#39;s GC. <code>cuNumeric.disable_gc!()</code> turns this off.</p><p>To free temporaries without waiting for the GC, see <a href="./perf/accelerate"><code>@accelerate</code></a>. Source: <code>src/memory.jl</code>.</p>`,20)])])}const m=a(i,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
